  "answer_text": "Here is a comprehensive overview of **examples of preventive medicine practices**, organized by the three main categories:\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udd35 What Is Preventive Medicine?\n\nPreventive medicine is a holistic approach to healthcare that considers the whole patient and the myriad factors that influence their well-being. This medical specialty differs from traditional medicine in that its focus is **proactive rather than reactive**.\n\nThere are **3 main types** of preventive medicine: primary, secondary, and tertiary.\n\n---\n\n## 1\ufe0f\u20e3 Primary Prevention\n*(Preventing disease before it occurs)*\n\nPrimary prevention can be defined as an action or behavior that does not allow a disease or adverse event to occur. Examples include **immunization, smoking cessation, or initiation of an exercise program** with the goal of disease prevention.\n\nKey examples include:\n\n- **Vaccinations:** Vaccinations represent perhaps the single most effective preventive care measure in medical history. They have eradicated or dramatically reduced many diseases that once caused widespread suffering and death. Vaccines are important in building a child's immune system, and children must have shots against diseases like mumps and polio.\n\n- **Lifestyle Counseling:** Clinical preventive medicine physicians may provide counseling for unhealthy habits and run preventive health screenings. These practitioners may work with patients who would benefit from lifestyle changes, often encountering cases involving diabetes, smoking, or obesity.\n\n- **STI Prevention:** All STIs can be prevented, all can be effectively managed, and many can be cured. Due to new STI screening techniques and technologies, healthcare professionals are now better equipped than ever to help prevent the spread of STIs.\n\n---\n\n## 2\ufe0f\u20e3 Secondary Prevention\n*(Early detection of disease)*\n\nSecondary preventive medicine focuses on the early detection of illness. This gives the patient a better chance of getting treatment. Detecting the ailment early slows down its progression. Examples of treatments under this category are **newborn screening and cancer screening**.\n\nKey examples include:\n\n- **Cancer Screenings:** Advances in cancer detection led to improved screening for various malignancies, including the widespread use of **colonoscopy** in colorectal cancer screening, **mammography** in breast cancer screening, and **Pap smear and human papillomavirus (HPV) testing** in cervical cancer.\n\n- **Cardiovascular Screenings:** Screenings for **elevated cholesterol levels and hypertension** serve critical roles in preventing, identifying, and controlling cardiovascular diseases.\n\n- **Routine Blood Pressure & Weight Measurement:** The most commonly reported preventive practices among primary care physicians are **blood pressure measurement (99%)**, screening for smoking (95%), and brief advice for smokers (95%).\n\n- **Well-Child Visits:** Well-child visits provide an opportunity for a medical professional to examine a child holistically, assess their physical and emotional needs, support their growth and development, detect health issues early, and intervene quickly if necessary.\n\n---\n\n## 3\ufe0f\u20e3 Tertiary Prevention\n*(Managing and limiting the effects of existing disease)*\n\nTertiary preventive medicine aims to **limit the damage of a current illness**. Making the patient as comfortable as possible is part of this level of care. Future care and long-term treatment modalities can help the patient achieve health stability, helping the patient return to daily routines.\n\n---\n\n## \ud83d\udca1 Why Is Preventive Medicine Important?\n\nPracticing preventive medicine can lower costs, as 75% of the country's annual health spending goes toward chronic and largely preventable diseases. Heart disease and stroke, for example, are responsible for one-third of deaths in the US each year, costing the healthcare system approximately $216 billion annually while also resulting in $147 billion in lost workplace productivity.\n\nPreventive care encompasses a wide range of healthcare services designed to **maintain wellness, identify risk factors, and detect disease early** when treatment is most effective.\n\n---\n\n### Summary Table\n\n| Category | Goal | Examples |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Primary** | Prevent disease onset | Vaccines, smoking cessation, exercise, nutrition |\n| **Secondary** | Early detection | Cancer screenings, blood pressure checks, STI testing |\n| **Tertiary** | Limit disease damage | Chronic disease management, rehabilitation, long-term care |",
